L897: Admins can review actions and other basic information about the connectors in Workspace settings → Apps, and set user permissions using cite154†RBAC†rbac . Users can add the Atlassian Rovo from Settings → Apps & Connectors.
L898: The Atlassian Rovo connector is available to Enterprise, Edu, Business, Pro and Plus customers with connectors enabled for their workspace.
L899: # November 25, 2025
L900: ## Model updates for Enterprise and Edu
L901: GPT-5.1 Instant is now live in the model picker for ChatGPT Enterprise and Edu workspaces, and GPT-5.1 Auto and GPT-5.1 Thinking are no longer available as Early Access options.
L902: GPT-5.1 Pro is now available in Early Access for Enterprise and Edu. Admins and owners can enable access in your cite143†workspace's settings†chatgpt.com . GPT-5.1 Pro is intended for more complex tasks where more reasoning is helpful.
L903: New custom GPTs created in Enterprise and Edu will begin using GPT-5.1 as the default model. Existing custom GPTs are unchanged, and creators can still select a different model (depending on what’s enabled for the workspace and role).
L904: # November 24, 2025
L905: ## New MCP connectors available today
L906: We’re rolling out a new set of MCP access connectors built by partners and reviewed by OpenAI, including Amplitude, Fireflies, Vercel, Monday.com, Stripe, Hex, Egnyte, Alpaca, BioRender, Semrush, and Jam.dev. This is available to Enterprise, Edu, Business, Pro and Plus customers with connectors enabled for their workspace. These connectors expand the set of tools customers can bring into ChatGPT and will be followed by additional connectors on a rolling basis.
L907: The connectors released today are access connectors, which fetch content when a user asks a question, built using MCP. Admins continue to govern access to connectors using RBAC. Connectors remain default off for Enterprise plans, and default on for Business plans.
L908: Admins can review Actions and other basic information about these connectors in Workspace settings → Apps.
L909: # November 19, 2025
L910: ## Custom connectors in company knowledge
L911: Starting today, company knowledge will support custom MCP connectors with search/fetch functionality. Custom connectors fitting these criteria that are enabled for your organization will appear in the company knowledge experience for allowed users, giving your team a more complete, accurate, and company-specific context when using the feature.
L912: Admins/owners can continue to manage access to custom connectors through cite110†RBAC in Workspace settings.
L913: Learn more about cite155†company knowledge and cite156†custom MCP connectors .
L914: # November 12, 2025
L915: ## GPT-5.1 in ChatGPT
L916: We’re updating GPT-5 to GPT-5.1 Instant and GPT-5.1 Thinking, making answers both smarter and more conversational. GPT-5.1 Instant now uses light adaptive reasoning for tougher questions while staying fast, and GPT-5.1 Thinking adapts its thinking time more precisely for complex tasks with clearer, less jargony responses.
L917: GPT-5.1 Auto continues to route each query to the best model, and GPT-5 models remain available for three months under the Legacy models dropdown so you can compare and transition at your own pace.
L918: Please note that access to GPT-5.1 is by default disabled for ChatGPT Enterprise workspaces. Admins and owners can enable access in their cite143†workspace settings†chatgpt.com .
L919: # November 13, 2025
L920: ## Apps & Apps SDK now available to Enterprise/Edu plans
L921: ChatGPT Enterprise/Edu customers will now have access to ChatGPT apps. Apps include interactive interfaces – like presentations, maps, and playlists – that respond to natural language and adapt to your conversation. You can start with a bulleted outline and ask Canva to turn it into a professional presentation, or turn a rough sketch into a FigJam diagram—all without leaving ChatGPT.
L922: Our pilot partners—like Figma, Canva, Booking.com, Coursera, Expedia, Spotify, and Zillow—will be available to Enterprise/Edu customers in markets where their services are offered, starting in English, with more apps coming.
L923: You can also build your own apps with the cite157†Apps SDK , then test and deploy it for your team using cite158†developer mode . Get started in the docs. Learn more: cite151†Apps in ChatGPT and the Apps SDK and cite157†Build with the Apps SDK L924: ## Synced Connector Updates
L925: ChatGPT Enterprise/Edu workspaces can now use ChatGPT connectors for cite159†Azure Boards , cite160†Basecamp , and cite161†Zoho CRM These connectors deliver faster, higher-quality answers–especially for knowledge-heavy prompts like strategy summaries, policy lookups, and internal research.
L926: For Enterprise/Edu workspaces, connectors are disabled by default— admins and owners can enable specific connectors at any time from their cite149†Workspace connectors settings†chatgpt.com , and configure connector access through cite110†RBAC . Individual users can connect enabled connectors they want to use from Settings > Apps & Connectors. Once enabled, ChatGPT will automatically reference the indexed content when relevant. Learn more about how to use connectors cite152†here .
L927: # November 6, 2025
L928: ## Custom connector action controls
L929: Admins and Owners can now enable or disable individual actions for custom connectors (e.g., to allow read but not write) and manually refresh actions to pick up developer updates. This brings finer control over what connectors can do in ChatGPT and a simple review path when new connector capabilities are added. Find these controls in Workspace Settings → Connectors → Manage actions or during publish.
L930: Available to ChatGPT Enterprise and Edu workspaces using custom connectors. Toggles apply at the workspace level; new actions are disabled by default until approved and modified actions keep their prior state. To use Refresh, an admin/owner must connect to the connector as a user.
L931: cite162†Learn more about these controls in our Help Center article .
L932: # October 27, 2025
L933: ## Synced Connector Updates
L934: Enterprise and Edu workspaces can now use ChatGPT connectors for cite163†Aha! , cite164†Asana , cite165†ClickUp , cite166†GitLab Issues , cite167†Help Scout , cite168†Teamwork.com , and cite169†Zoho Desk . Synced connectors deliver faster, higher-quality answers – especially for knowledge-heavy prompts like strategy summaries, policy lookups, and internal research.
L935: Admins and Owners can enable connectors from their cite149†Workspace connectors settings†chatgpt.com , and can configure user access using cite110†RBAC . Connectors remain off by default until an Admin or Owner turns them on. Individual users can connect enabled connectors they want to use from Settings > Apps & Connectors. Once enabled, ChatGPT will automatically reference the indexed content when relevant. Learn more about how to use connectors cite152†here .
L936: # October 23, 2025
L937: ## Company knowledge in ChatGPT
L938: Company knowledge is a new way to bring together context from all your cite152†connected tools for answers that know your business, with citations and links back to sources from your apps (for example: Slack, SharePoint, Google Drive, GitHub, Notion, HubSpot, Zendesk, Azure DevOps, Asana, and more).
L939: Company knowledge respects your existing company permissions, so ChatGPT only has access to what each user is already authorized to view. It follows the role-based permissions admins have set for each connector. OpenAI never trains on your data by default. cite170†Learn more†openai.com about OpenAI's enterprise-grade security, compliance, and data privacy programs.
L940: Admins enable connectors for the workspace and can manage access with cite110†RBAC . At least one connector must be enabled for a user to see company knowledge.
L941: To use it, start a chat, select company knowledge under the message composer, and ask your question to see cited results from approved connectors. Learn more in our cite155†Help Center article .
L942: # October 17, 2025
L943: ## Build, upload, and publish MCP connectors in ChatGPT [Beta]
L944: We’re rolling out full Model Context Protocol (MCP) support with developer mode so your organization can build, test, and publish MCP-powered cite152†connectors with read/write capabilities that let ChatGPT take action in your tools. Kick off workflows, create project management tasks, update your CRM, or combine connectors for more complex orchestrations.
L945: Admins can review and publish connectors to the workspace, and use cite110†RBAC to control who can develop, test, and use each connector. Build your own MCP connector, or upload trusted third party connectors.
L946: Learn more in our cite158†Help Center article .
L947: # October 16, 2025
L948: ## Updated Workspace settings
L949: We’ve updated Workspace settings to make administration clearer and faster. A new General tab now centralizes workspace customization (name, logo, appearance, and workspace-wide instructions) and adds an AI policy modal you can configure to remind users of your organization’s AI policies on a recurring basis.
L950: We’ve also streamlined navigation by combining Identity & provisioning with IP allowlists into a new Identity & access tab, and by renaming Settings & permissions to Permissions & roles.
L951: See cite171†What workspace settings can I control for my workspace for details.
L952: ## Enterprise Key Management (EKM)
L953: Enterprise Key Management (EKM) is now available for Enterprise and Edu customers. This capability allows organizations to encrypt all customer content using their own external Key Management System (KMS), giving organizations greater control over data security and compliance.
L954: EKM supports integrations with Google Cloud Platform (GCP), Amazon Web Services (AWS), and Microsoft Azure.
L955: Read our cite61†OpenAI Enterprise Key Management (EKM) Overview to get started.
L1211: # June 4, 2025
L1212: 
L1213: ## Connectors in ChatGPT (Beta)
L1214: 
L1215: Customers can now enable connectors to bring internal tools and content into ChatGPT.
L1216: 
L1217:   * Supported connectors: Google Drive, SharePoint, Dropbox, and Box
L1218: 
L1219:   * Real-time access with in-line citations
L1220: 
L1221:   * Admins control connector access in Admin Settings (off by default)
L1222: 
L1223: Learn more about cite83†Connectors in ChatGPT .
L1224: ## Connectors in deep research (Beta)
L1225: 
L1226: Use connectors in Deep Research to generate long-form, cited responses that include your company’s internal tools.
L1227: 
L1228:   * Supported connectors: Google Drive, SharePoint, Dropbox, Box, Outlook, Gmail, Google Calendar, Linear, GitHub, HubSpot, and Teams
L1229: 
L1230:   * Combines internal + web sources for synthesis
L1231: 
L1232: Learn more about cite83†Connectors in ChatGPT .
L1233: ## Google Drive synced connector
L1234: 
L1235: The synced connector indexes your organization’s Drive content in advance, enabling fast and high quality contextual responses.
L1236: 
L1237:   * Semantic search across Docs, Slides, Sheets, and more in ChatGPT
L1238: 
L1239:   * Supports o4-mini, o3, and GPT-4o
L1240: 
L1241:   * Admin scoping by user
L1242: 
L1243: Learn more about the cite196†Google Drive synced connector .
L1244: ## Custom connectors via Model Context Protocol (Beta)
L1245: 
L1246: Admins can now build and deploy custom connectors to proprietary systems using Model Context Protocol (MCP).
L1247: 
L1248:   * Requires a remote MCP server
L1249: 
L1250:   * Available only in deep research
L1251: 
L1252:   * Admin-published connectors appear in the connector list for all users
L1253: 
L1254: Learn more about cite219†building custom connectors with MCP†platform.openai.com .

